Inquiring minds want to know what to do with sinks.

One nifty trick has to do with sinks' ability to disgorge black
puddings.  Black puddings, when attacked, often divide in two.  Black
puddings, when killed, give a pretty good number of experience points.
And black pudding corpses, when eaten, often yield intrinsics.

So a useful strategy for climbing up in experience quickly is to drop
a scroll of Frighten Monster next to a sink and kick the sink until a
black pudding shows up.  Then hit on the pudding and its spawn until
you get to level 10 experience.  Eat corpses as digestion allows; you
will want to wear any rings that you have that will increase your
digestion, to allow you to eat as many of the corpses as you can.

Some hints on this-- it is a good idea to enscribe Elbereth at the
exits to the rooms where the sink is.  This keeps the pudding near
enough that they come by where you can hit them from the scroll from
time to time.  Also, because they multiply so quickly, it's easy to
not realize that you missed one pudding that is hanging out in the
halls, and they do nasty things to armor.

The one thing that can kill you quickly in this position is if a well
armed group of "@"'s come into the room -- you won't be able to leave
the scroll (cuz the puddings block your way and are nasty) and the
"@"'s (werewolves, elves, soldiers, whatever) can surround you and hit
you ("@"'s are not frightened by the scroll.)  Therefore, place the
scroll in such a way that you could probably get a couple good shots
of your favorite ray weapon into such a party.  Or have some other
protection for such an eventuality (teleport scroll, ring of
conflict.)  And of course, even on the scroll you can be hit by thrown
objects, but this hasn't really been a severe problem.  It might be if
a hoard of Uruk-hai came in shooting poison arrows.  However, after
running the pudding mill for a while you'll probably get poison
resistance. 

Of course, this whole scene can be a real winner if you've got a
nearby alter to sacrifice the pudding corpses you can't eat.  But
you've got to get lucky for that.  You also will find some neat prizes
left in the piles of pudding corpses.

You DON'T want to have a pet in the room under such circumstances.
If you step off the scroll to pick up a corpse and your dog picks up
the scroll, you could be embarrassed.  Take the pet to another level
and leave it there.  You can always retame it by throwing it a fresh
black pudding corpse later.

I'm playing 3.0p6, so this may have changed slightly in later
versions.  And of course there are many variations on the scroll
(repeatedly enscribing Elbereth, levitating over Elbereth, letting a
ring of conflict keep them from attacking you.)  One thing to keep in
mind if you're levitating over Elbereth is that sinks knock off rings
and boots of levitation.

